  retention policy inside a task scoped as "add logging." If you already have a log endpoint I don't know about, option
  2 becomes the right answer.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where should logs go in production? → Transport seam, console now (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the one that made me flag [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m.

  [1mThe fork — how sensitive fields stay out of the logs.[0m Right now [38;5;153mlogin()[39m logs a username, and a password sits in the
  same scope. Once a logger exists and is easy to call, the chance of a credential landing in a log line goes up, not
  down. Where you put the guard determines what a mistake costs.

  - [1mDenylist in the logger[0m — logger scrubs known-sensitive keys ([38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153msecret[39m, …) from any object it's
    handed. Catches accidents without making callers think. Fails open: a field nobody listed gets logged verbatim.
  - [1mAllowlist in the logger[0m — only explicitly permitted keys are serialized; everything else becomes [38;5;153m[redacted][39m. Fails
    closed, which is the right default for credentials, but it's friction on every new log call and tends to get worked
    around with string interpolation.
  - [1mCaller's responsibility, no redaction[0m — simplest logger, all discipline lives in code review. Given this repo logs a
    username next to a password today, I don't think that discipline is there to rely on.

  I'd recommend the [1mdenylist[0m: it fixes the actual bug in front of us ([38;5;153mconsole.log("Logging in:", username)[39m), costs
  nothing at call sites, and can be tightened to an allowlist later. An allowlist is the more defensible choice if you
  expect real user data to flow through these logs.
